This is a "plugin" for the Video Disk Recorder (VDR).

Written by:                  Andreas Baierl <ichgeh@imkreisrum.de>

Project's homepage:          https://github.com/rellla/vdr-plugin-vdrsternelec

Latest version available at: https://github.com/rellla/vdr-plugin-vdrsternelec

This program is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify
it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
the Free Software Foundation; either version 2 of the License, or
(at your option) any later version.
See the file COPYING for more information.


Description
-----------

vdrsternelec updates a VDR*ELEC system (VDR on LibreELEC or CoreELEC) from
within VDR, the same way the LibreELEC settings add-on does it in Kodi:

  - it asks one or more release servers for the releases available for the
    device it is running on,
  - downloads the selected update file (.tar or .img.gz) into
    /storage/.update and verifies its SHA-256 checksum,
  - and reboots the system, which makes LibreELEC/CoreELEC install the update.

Updates can be selected in the OSD, controlled via SVDRP, or checked for (and
optionally downloaded and installed) automatically.

The device is identified by <DEVICE>.<ARCH> (e.g. "RPi4.aarch64"), read from
LIBREELEC_ARCH or COREELEC_ARCH in /etc/os-release. Only files whose name
contains this string are offered.


Requirements
------------

  - VDR >= 2.4.0
  - libcurl
  - libsmbclient (optional, for Samba shares; built automatically if found,
    "make SMB=0" disables it)


Installation
------------

  make
  make install

Command line options:

  -o FILE, --os-release=FILE  read the system information from FILE
                              (default: /etc/os-release)


Release servers
---------------

Servers are configured in the OSD ("VDR*ELEC update" -> "Release servers...")
or in the file servers.conf in the plugin's configuration directory
(usually /storage/.config/vdropt/plugins/vdrsternelec/servers.conf on
VDR*ELEC). If the file does not exist, the official VDR*ELEC releases on
GitHub (Zabrimus/VDRSternELEC) are used.

One server per line:

  [!]type|name|url|channel|filter

  type     json    A LibreELEC style release server providing a
                   releases.json (the format used by the LibreELEC settings
                   add-on and the USB-SD creator). url is the base URL of
                   the server or the full URL of the releases.json.
           github  GitHub releases. url is "owner/repo" or the URL of the
                   repository.
           index   A plain HTTP directory listing (e.g. from nginx, Apache
                   or "python3 -m http.server") containing the update files.
           dir     A local directory or a mounted network share containing
                   the update files ("manual update"). url is the absolute
                   path, e.g. /storage/updates or /media/nas/vdrelec.
           smb     A Samba (Windows) share, accessed directly without
                   mounting. url is
                   smb://[[domain;]user[:password]@]server/share[/path]
                   Special characters in user or password must be
                   percent-encoded (e.g. @ as %40).
  name     Name shown in the OSD.
  channel  json:   name of the channel to use (e.g. "LibreELEC-13.0").
           github: only tags containing this text are used
                   (e.g. "LibreELEC-13" or "CoreELEC-21").
           dir, smb: as json, if the directory contains a releases.json.
           Empty means all channels/tags. Not used for index.
  filter   Optional regular expression (ECMAScript syntax). If given, it is
           used instead of the device string to select the update files.
  !        A leading '!' disables the entry.

Examples (see also servers.conf.example):

  github|VDR*ELEC|Zabrimus/VDRSternELEC|LibreELEC-13|
  json|My builds|https://builds.example.org/vdrelec/||
  index|NAS|http://192.168.1.10/vdrelec/||
  dir|USB stick|/var/media/USBSTICK/updates||
  smb|NAS|smb://user:secret@192.168.1.10/vdr/updates||

Checksums are taken from releases.json, from the "digest" GitHub provides for
release assets, from a file <update file>.sha256 next to the update file, or
from a checksum list named SHA256SUMS (or sha256sums, sha256sums.txt) in the
same directory, as created by

  sha256sum *.tar *.img.gz > SHA256SUMS

Files without checksum are refused unless "Require checksum" is disabled.


Local directories and Samba shares (manual update)
--------------------------------------------------

A "dir" or "smb" server lists the update files of a directory, just like
copying a file into /storage/.update manually, but with checksum verification
and selection in the OSD. Only files directly in that directory are listed,
and only files for this device (or matching the filter). The release date is
the modification time of the file.

If the directory contains a releases.json (or the path points directly to a
.json file), it is used instead of the file list, exactly like on a
"json" server. This way a copy of a release server can be used from a USB
stick or a share. The files are always taken relative to the directory of the
JSON file ("url" entries in the JSON file are ignored), and paths leaving that
directory ("..") are refused.

Samba shares can be used directly with the "smb" type:

  smb|NAS|smb://user:secret@nas/vdr/updates||
  smb|NAS guest|smb://nas/public/vdrelec||

Without user name, the share is accessed anonymously. The password is stored
in servers.conf in plain text; the SVDRP command SERVERS shows it as "***",
and it is only sent to the server of that entry.

Other network shares (e.g. NFS) must be mounted first, e.g. with a systemd
mount unit in /storage/.config/system.d (see the LibreELEC wiki on mounting
network shares), and the mount point is used with the "dir" type:

  dir|NAS|/storage/nas/vdrelec||

Removable media appear under /var/media/<label>. The update directory itself
can't be used as source.


Build date
----------

The OSD shows the build date of the installed system and of the update. For
the installed system it is taken from the version (LibreELEC devel builds
contain the build time, nightly builds the date) or from the time of
/etc/os-release. For releases it is taken from the file name, the time given
by the server (releases.json, GitHub, file time) or a date in the tag.

Note: unauthenticated access to the GitHub API is limited to 60 requests per
hour and IP address.


Which release is newer?
-----------------------

Releases are compared with the installed system in this order:

  1. If the plugin itself downloaded the installed update, it knows the file
     and its date (stored in the file "state" in the plugin's configuration
     directory).
  2. Dates contained in the version (e.g. 13.0-nightly-20261020-abc1234).
  3. Version numbers (e.g. 12.0.1 < 12.0.2).
  4. The date of the release (build time, upload time or a date contained in
     the GitHub tag) compared with the build time of the installed system
     (modification time of /etc/os-release).

Releases are marked with "*" (newer) and "=" (installed) in the OSD and in
the SVDRP command LIST.


Setup options
-------------

  Hide main menu entry        Hide the plugin's entry in VDR's main menu.
  Check for updates           Check the servers for new releases
  automatically               automatically (default: off, see "Network
                              access" below). The following three options
                              are only shown if this is enabled:
    Check interval (h)        Hours between the checks. The first check is
                              done 5 minutes after VDR has started (or right
                              after enabling the option).
    Notify about new          Show an OSD message when the automatic check
    releases                  finds a new release (once per release).
    Download new releases     Download a new release found by the automatic
    automatically             check into the update directory.
  Reboot to install           "manually": reboot via the OSD or SVDRP.
                              "automatically when idle": reboot as soon as
                              an update is waiting and VDR is idle (no
                              recording, no timer within the next 15 minutes,
                              no OSD open, no key pressed for the idle time,
                              no plugin busy).
  Idle time before reboot     See above.
  Require checksum            Refuse update files without checksum.
  Show older releases         Also list releases older than the installed
                              one (downgrade).
  Show pre-releases           Also list GitHub pre-releases.
  Update directory            Where update files are stored
                              (default: /storage/.update).
  Reboot command              Command used to reboot (default: reboot).
  U-Boot board                For u-boot based devices with board specific
                              images in releases.json (e.g. "odroid-c2").
                              Empty lists all boards.
  Architecture                Overrides the device string from os-release.


Network access
--------------

By default the plugin does not access any server on its own. Servers are only
contacted when the user checks for updates (OSD, SVDRP CHECK) or installs a
release. Only the automatic check, which has to be enabled in the setup
("Check for updates automatically"), contacts the servers periodically.

The plugin only connects to the servers in the server list. Without a
servers.conf this is the GitHub API (api.github.com) for the releases of
Zabrimus/VDRSternELEC, and the downloads come from github.com. Requests carry
the User-Agent "vdr-plugin-vdrsternelec/<version>"; nothing else about the
system is sent (the device string is only used locally to select the files).
To use only local sources, replace the default server in the server list.


SVDRP commands
--------------

  STAT              Installed version, build dates and state of the updater.
  CHECK             Check all enabled servers (runs in the background).
  LIST [NEW]        Releases found by the last check (NEW: only newer ones).
  INSTALL <number>  Download and verify release <number> (see LIST).
  CANCEL            Cancel a running download.
  DISCARD           Remove a downloaded, not yet installed update.
  REBOOT            Reboot now to install a downloaded update.
  SERVERS           List the configured servers.

Example:

  svdrpsend PLUG vdrsternelec CHECK
  svdrpsend PLUG vdrsternelec LIST NEW
  svdrpsend PLUG vdrsternelec INSTALL 1
  svdrpsend PLUG vdrsternelec STAT
  svdrpsend PLUG vdrsternelec REBOOT


Notes
-----

  - Only one update file is kept in the update directory, other update files
    found there are removed when a new update has been downloaded.
  - While a download is running, VDR does not shut down automatically.
  - A backup of /storage before updating is always a good idea.
